WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.160
- I have lupus.

00:00:01.160 --> 00:00:02.403
- I have type one diabetes.

00:00:02.403 --> 00:00:03.432
- I have Hashimoto's.

00:00:03.432 --> 00:00:06.480
- I have ankylosing spondylitis
and Crohn's disease.

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:13.140
One day, it was December 19th
like I'll never forget it

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:17.330
and I just woke up and my
right foot was really swollen.

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:19.820
- Honestly throughout
my life I've had like

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:23.390
health issues, I've had
periods where my body

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:25.760
has just shut down and I couldn't move,

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:29.940
I got rashes throughout
my entire adolescence,

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:32.520
and I just thought that was just me.

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:33.840
- And I just thought
it was stress related.

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:35.800
I remember just going through
one of the most stressful

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:37.290
semesters I've had in college.

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:38.680
- I was feeling really manic.

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:40.550
Like I thought I was
going through an episode

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:43.580
cause for a few months I
would feel like really good,

00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:45.670
and have a lot of energy,
and just racing thoughts,

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:47.380
and it was like just kinda jittery,

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:48.320
and I was losing weight,

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:49.630
and I felt like oh that's great.

00:00:49.630 --> 00:00:51.050
And then I would be eating the same food

00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:54.810
and a few months later I
would be really like tired,

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:55.940
and I would feel really emotional,

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:57.560
and really depressed, and
I'd be gaining weight,

00:00:57.560 --> 00:00:59.790
and I didn't really change
anything in my lifestyle.

00:00:59.790 --> 00:01:01.370
- My mom took me to urgent care

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:03.430
and they did an X-ray and they said

00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:05.170
I had a hairline fracture.

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:07.270
To this day like I don't think I did

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:08.670
but I don't really know.

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:11.320
So they gave me this
little butt-booty thing

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:13.330
to walk around in and
you're supposed to wear it

00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:14.320
for like a few weeks,

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:16.750
and after those few weeks
nothing had changed,

00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:18.270
and it had actually kind of gotten worse.

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:20.460
My foot was more swollen
and now my big toe

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:22.090
is also really swollen and hurt,

00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:23.660
and that was kind of
just like the beginning

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:27.560
of a six month process of
going to dozens of doctors,

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:30.570
and getting like so many
different diagnoses.

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:32.640
- Well by the time I was in college,

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:35.340
I was getting sick a ton

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:38.000
and after I'd graduated,

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:39.730
I went through a period where I just

00:01:39.730 --> 00:01:40.850
could not move my body.

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:43.070
It literally felt like I had been run over

00:01:43.070 --> 00:01:45.990
by a train multiple times.

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:48.050
People would be like
well what's feeling bad

00:01:48.050 --> 00:01:49.990
and I'm like ah everything.

00:01:49.990 --> 00:01:52.350
- The only signs or
symptoms that I was getting

00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:54.410
where I was just thirsty a lot,

00:01:54.410 --> 00:01:56.890
like all the time, like I
just needed a lot of fluids

00:01:56.890 --> 00:01:58.910
and so by extension I was
just like peeing a lot.

00:01:58.910 --> 00:02:00.200
- And I went to the
doctor and they thought

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:01.460
oh well you're like depressed,

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:02.880
we're maybe gonna give you some pills

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:04.700
cause I just, I was crying
in the doctors office,

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:05.890
I was crying everywhere I went.

00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:07.850
I did my physical and my results came back

00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:09.840
and my thyroid was all off.

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:12.040
- You know a doctor told
my mum that I had leukemia

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:13.860
while I was in the room and just like

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:16.430
so many other random diagnoses,

00:02:16.430 --> 00:02:19.730
until finally my mum took
me to a rheumatologist

00:02:19.730 --> 00:02:21.450
who diagnosed me at the time

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:23.560
with juvenile rheumatoid arthritis.

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:25.460
- Well it wasn't until my mum told me

00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:28.410
that lupus actually runs in my family

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:29.690
and I should get tested for it

00:02:29.690 --> 00:02:30.960
cause I had been to doctors,

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:34.680
they never seem to test
autoimmune diseases first.

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:37.310
So I had to literally beg them please

00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:39.000
just like run some tests for it.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.080
- For a few years I thought
okay I'm just living

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:42.410
with this arthritis thing,

00:02:42.410 --> 00:02:44.780
and then my freshman year of high school,

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:46.800
I started having lots of stomach issues.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:50.890
I had a colonoscopy and I
was diagnosed with colitis.

00:02:50.890 --> 00:02:52.608
- It was only until
like I went on vacation

00:02:52.608 --> 00:02:54.900
after the semester ended
with my family and stuff,

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:57.350
and my mom actually started
noticing those signs

00:02:57.350 --> 00:02:58.634
of just being thirsty all of the time,

00:02:58.634 --> 00:02:59.950
so she was just like you should check

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:01.650
to see if you have type one diabetes.

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:03.840
I went to my like school doctor after that

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:06.390
and they did some tests,
sugar levels and stuff,

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:07.350
urine and everything,

00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:09.260
they were saying yeah this is like signs

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:10.290
of type one diabetes.

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:14.160
- Having the colitis, the
juvenile rheumatoid arthritis,

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:17.960
and the iritis, my doctor
was like oh actually,

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:19.830
because of all of these different things,

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:21.330
we're gonna test you for something called

00:03:21.330 --> 00:03:26.330
the HLA-B27 gene, and they
tested me for that and I had it,

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:28.750
and that's when they sort
of changed my diagnosis

00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:30.350
to ankylosing spondylitis.

00:03:30.350 --> 00:03:32.320
- I did my physical and
my results came back

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:33.570
and my thyroid was all off.

00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:35.300
So they started giving me more thyroid.

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:37.020
When you find out that
you like having something

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:39.030
wrong with you when you
feel like something's wrong,

00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:40.890
it just makes you feel so much better

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:41.730
because it just confirms like

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:43.280
nothing's actually wrong with you,

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:45.240
it's just like your body is messing up.

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:47.020
- I'm not a super-emotional person

00:03:47.020 --> 00:03:49.970
but when I got my diagnosis, I cried.

00:03:49.970 --> 00:03:52.830
Half out of relief that I knew like

00:03:52.830 --> 00:03:54.160
something was wrong with me,

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:57.630
and half out of I don't know
what the fuck is going on.

00:03:57.630 --> 00:04:00.090
People don't really talk
about autoimmune diseases

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:02.550
so like I didn't really
know much about it.

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:04.330
- Imagine if we were
living in a world already

00:04:04.330 --> 00:04:06.280
of like driverless cars and that's like

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:08.110
been your whole life, and
you're just used to that,

00:04:08.110 --> 00:04:09.310
and then all of a sudden you wake up

00:04:09.310 --> 00:04:11.340
and then now you have a stick-shift,

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:13.110
and you have to like do this manually.

00:04:13.110 --> 00:04:15.710
It's like oh my god, I had
no idea that there were

00:04:15.710 --> 00:04:17.070
all these components to this,

00:04:17.070 --> 00:04:19.140
like all these components to life,

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:20.040
and just basic survival.

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:22.620
- I might have a really
good couple of weeks

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:23.810
and be feeling great,

00:04:23.810 --> 00:04:26.860
and then just the next
three weeks just be sick,

00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:29.220
just like every single
day and feel terrible.

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:31.910
And also emotional things
have been hard for me

00:04:31.910 --> 00:04:33.050
to come to terms with

00:04:33.050 --> 00:04:35.970
because one of the symptoms is depression,

00:04:35.970 --> 00:04:38.700
and I battled with depression all my life,

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:42.020
and it's hard when you
physically feel sick

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:44.840
and you emotionally,
and mentally feel sick,

00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:47.740
and just having a support
system behind you,

00:04:47.740 --> 00:04:50.170
guiding you through that because trust me

00:04:50.170 --> 00:04:54.000
when you feel like you
can't get out of bed

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:56.780
and walk over to the bathroom
cause you're in pain,

00:04:56.780 --> 00:04:59.380
like that will fuck
things up in your head,

00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:01.830
and just making sure you're feeling loved

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:03.747
and supported is like
the most important thing.

00:05:03.747 --> 00:05:04.950
- You need to have self-care.

00:05:04.950 --> 00:05:06.720
I think the doctor talked to me about

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:09.920
it was probably brought on
by that stressful episode

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:12.940
of my life that I had
because a lot of stress

00:05:12.940 --> 00:05:14.990
can do a lot of damage to your body.

00:05:14.990 --> 00:05:17.550
So I think kind of
getting diagnosed with it

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:20.080
it made me think like
self-care is really important,

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:22.260
and it's just made me really
in tuned with my body,

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:23.600
that everything works together

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:26.398
like this little gland here like affects

00:05:26.398 --> 00:05:28.932
my hair, and like sleep, and fatigue,

00:05:28.932 --> 00:05:31.160
and like body pains, and things like that.

00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:32.293
- It takes some time to adjust

00:05:32.293 --> 00:05:34.230
but you sort of start to just learn

00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:36.600
and adapt, and figure
out those extra steps

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:37.433
that you need to take.

00:05:37.433 --> 00:05:39.250
- First and foremost like I'm Macy

00:05:39.250 --> 00:05:42.750
and I love to travel, and I love X, Y, Z,

00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:44.600
and like I'm not this disease.

00:05:44.600 --> 00:05:47.260
Like that's like so far down in like

00:05:47.260 --> 00:05:49.020
the list of attributes that I have.

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:51.690
I think that like if you
can find the right medicine

00:05:51.690 --> 00:05:52.750
that works for you,

00:05:52.750 --> 00:05:55.300
you can live a life just like anyone else.

00:05:55.300 --> 00:05:57.500
Like there's literally
nothing in the whole world

00:05:57.500 --> 00:06:00.960
that I can't do because of this disease.

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:02.870
- If you get Hashimoto's, don't panic.

00:06:02.870 --> 00:06:04.870
It's actually good that
you know that you have it.

00:06:04.870 --> 00:06:06.270
You can kinda take the control back

00:06:06.270 --> 00:06:07.550
and improve your life.

00:06:07.550 --> 00:06:09.870
- This is not a death sentence.

00:06:09.870 --> 00:06:13.010
It sucks because you might not know

00:06:13.010 --> 00:06:14.740
a lot about what's going on.

00:06:14.740 --> 00:06:17.520
I filled some time with research,

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:20.870
I just tried to learn, and
also listen to yourself,

00:06:20.870 --> 00:06:24.100
like I still go out, I still have fun,

00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:26.080
but you like need to know your limits

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:28.450
of what your body is capable of.

00:06:28.450 --> 00:06:30.310
And talk to people.

00:06:30.310 --> 00:06:32.010
You do not have to go about this alone

00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:33.310
cause you are not alone.

00:06:33.310 --> 00:06:36.990
There are plenty of chat
rooms, and Facebook groups,

00:06:36.990 --> 00:06:39.440
and all sorts of things where
you can meet other people

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:41.340
who are suffering from the same thing.

